1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,685
It's day eleven. You haven't slept for two

2
00:00:02,685 --> 00:00:04,470
hundred and sixty-four hours.

3
00:00:05,020 --> 00:00:06,490
Your eyes won't focus.

4
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:08,360
Your words come out slurred.

5
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:10,965
Somewhere in your brain, little patches

6
00:00:10,965 --> 00:00:14,038
are switching off by themselves, a few seconds

7
00:00:14,038 --> 00:00:16,510
at a time, and you don't even notice.

8
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,919
You're seeing things that aren't there.

9
00:00:18,919 --> 00:00:20,826
And you're pretty sure the people around

10
00:00:20,826 --> 00:00:21,970
you are up to something.

11
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:24,313
And here's the strange part.

12
00:00:24,313 --> 00:00:27,003
Back then, scientists honestly didn't know

13
00:00:27,003 --> 00:00:28,476
if this would kill you.

14
00:00:28,476 --> 00:00:31,102
In just over a week, a seventeen year old

15
00:00:31,102 --> 00:00:33,663
was going to find out, live, in front of

16
00:00:33,663 --> 00:00:34,880
the national press.

17
00:00:35,430 --> 00:00:37,408
So let's do it properly.

18
00:00:37,408 --> 00:00:39,633
You. No sleep. Eleven days.

19
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:43,177
Hour by hour, organ by organ, with a vitals

20
00:00:43,177 --> 00:00:46,638
monitor that will slowly stop being funny.

21
00:00:46,638 --> 00:00:47,380
Let's go.

22
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:51,064
San Diego, December nineteen sixty-three.

23
00:00:51,064 --> 00:00:54,121
Two high school boys need a science fair

24
00:00:54,121 --> 00:00:57,101
project. Their idea? Stay awake as long

25
00:00:57,101 --> 00:00:58,630
as humanly possible.

26
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:01,906
They flip a coin to decide who has to do

27
00:01:01,906 --> 00:01:03,337
it. One of them wins.

28
00:01:03,337 --> 00:01:05,449
The other one is Randy Gardner.

29
00:01:05,449 --> 00:01:06,880
Today, you are Randy.

30
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:10,486
The record to beat belongs to a radio DJ

31
00:01:10,486 --> 00:01:13,619
in Honolulu. Two hundred and sixty hours.

32
00:01:13,619 --> 00:01:15,376
Just under eleven days.

33
00:01:15,376 --> 00:01:18,050
Easy. You've stayed up late before.

34
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,015
The rules are simple.

35
00:01:20,015 --> 00:01:21,631
No pills, no stimulants.

36
00:01:21,631 --> 00:01:24,663
Your helpers later say you had the occasional

37
00:01:24,663 --> 00:01:26,010
Coke, and that's it.

38
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,132
The first night feels like a sleepover.

39
00:01:29,132 --> 00:01:31,969
Then the sun comes up, and your body starts

40
00:01:31,969 --> 00:01:33,090
sending invoices.

41
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:36,219
At around seventeen to nineteen hours awake,

42
00:01:36,219 --> 00:01:38,506
researchers found that people performed

43
00:01:38,506 --> 00:01:40,850
on some tests as badly as someone with a

44
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,371
blood alcohol level of zero point zero five

45
00:01:43,371 --> 00:01:43,840
percent.

46
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:46,744
Reaction times on some tests were up to

47
00:01:46,744 --> 00:01:49,098
fifty percent slower. You haven't had a

48
00:01:49,098 --> 00:01:51,633
single drink. Your brain just behaves like

49
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:52,720
it's had a couple.

50
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:55,513
And keep going a few more hours, and it

51
00:01:55,513 --> 00:01:57,812
got worse, as bad as the highest alcohol

52
00:01:57,812 --> 00:02:00,055
dose they tested. That's why you're not

53
00:02:00,055 --> 00:02:02,470
allowed anywhere near a car in this video.

54
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,138
Twenty-four hours. A whole day.

55
00:02:05,138 --> 00:02:08,213
And deep in your brain, a small almond-shaped

56
00:02:08,213 --> 00:02:11,152
alarm system called the amygdala is getting

57
00:02:11,152 --> 00:02:11,630
louder.

58
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,754
In one brain scan study, people who'd been

59
00:02:14,754 --> 00:02:17,512
kept awake for about a day and a half reacted

60
00:02:17,512 --> 00:02:19,903
to upsetting pictures with around sixty

61
00:02:19,903 --> 00:02:22,477
percent more amygdala activity than people

62
00:02:22,477 --> 00:02:23,090
who slept.

63
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:27,017
Meanwhile, the part of your brain that normally

64
00:02:27,017 --> 00:02:29,963
says, calm down, it's just a toaster, has

65
00:02:29,963 --> 00:02:32,909
gone quiet. So everything feels personal.

66
00:02:32,909 --> 00:02:34,130
Even the toaster.

67
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:36,915
And you're hungry. Weirdly hungry.

68
00:02:36,915 --> 00:02:39,543
Short sleep is linked to lower levels of

69
00:02:39,543 --> 00:02:42,304
leptin, the hormone that says you're full,

70
00:02:42,304 --> 00:02:45,064
and higher ghrelin, the hormone that says,

71
00:02:45,064 --> 00:02:45,590
feed me.

72
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:48,637
That's why at three in the morning a salad

73
00:02:48,637 --> 00:02:51,075
has never once called your name, but cold

74
00:02:51,075 --> 00:02:51,670
pizza has.

75
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:54,792
Day two. Now the strangest thing starts

76
00:02:54,792 --> 00:02:57,627
happening. Your brain begins to fall asleep

77
00:02:57,627 --> 00:02:58,880
without asking you.

78
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:01,485
These are called microsleeps.

79
00:03:01,485 --> 00:03:04,814
Tiny episodes of sleep that last a few seconds,

80
00:03:04,814 --> 00:03:06,515
sometimes up to fifteen.

81
00:03:06,515 --> 00:03:09,915
From the inside, it feels like nothing happened.

82
00:03:09,915 --> 00:03:11,970
The world just skips forward.

83
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,547
At a desk, that's embarrassing.

84
00:03:14,547 --> 00:03:17,425
On a road, it's how a car travels the length

85
00:03:17,425 --> 00:03:20,434
of a football field with nobody really driving

86
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:20,630
it.

87
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:23,687
So your friends keep you moving.

88
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:26,978
Basketball. Bowling. Anything. Because the

89
00:03:26,978 --> 00:03:30,347
moment you close your eyes, you are asleep.

90
00:03:30,347 --> 00:03:31,130
Instantly.

91
00:03:31,680 --> 00:03:34,302
Funny detail. Your basketball actually gets

92
00:03:34,302 --> 00:03:37,046
better. Partly because you're fit, and partly

93
00:03:37,046 --> 00:03:39,425
because you're playing basketball every

94
00:03:39,425 --> 00:03:41,010
night instead of sleeping.

95
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:44,269
Day three. Your senses start to glitch.

96
00:03:44,269 --> 00:03:47,255
The scientists test your taste, your smell,

97
00:03:47,255 --> 00:03:50,380
your hearing, and things are getting strange.

98
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:53,319
You keep begging them, don't make me smell

99
00:03:53,319 --> 00:03:55,025
that, I can't stand the smell.

100
00:03:55,025 --> 00:03:57,300
Ordinary smells now feel like an attack.

101
00:03:57,850 --> 00:04:00,850
And here's what your brain is quietly missing.

102
00:04:00,850 --> 00:04:03,394
In studies on mice, sleep opened up the

103
00:04:03,394 --> 00:04:06,068
spaces between brain cells by about sixty

104
00:04:06,068 --> 00:04:08,611
percent, letting fluid wash through and

105
00:04:08,611 --> 00:04:09,720
carry waste away.

106
00:04:10,270 --> 00:04:12,797
Awake, that cleaning crew works much more

107
00:04:12,797 --> 00:04:15,509
slowly. One of the things it normally clears

108
00:04:15,509 --> 00:04:18,468
is beta amyloid, a protein linked to Alzheimer's

109
00:04:18,468 --> 00:04:20,995
disease. You are now three days behind on

110
00:04:20,995 --> 00:04:21,550
the bins.

111
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:24,630
By day four, things get properly weird.

112
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:27,848
Lieutenant Commander John Ross, the navy

113
00:04:27,848 --> 00:04:30,717
doctor checking your health, later reported

114
00:04:30,717 --> 00:04:33,318
moodiness, trouble concentrating, short

115
00:04:33,318 --> 00:04:36,720
term memory problems, paranoia, and hallucinations.

116
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:39,920
But the famous Stanford sleep scientist

117
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:42,705
who joins the experiment, William Dement,

118
00:04:42,705 --> 00:04:45,491
sees something else. On day ten, you beat

119
00:04:45,491 --> 00:04:46,510
him at pinball.

120
00:04:47,060 --> 00:04:48,081
So which is it?

121
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:50,736
Are you falling apart, or are you fine?

122
00:04:50,736 --> 00:04:52,574
The honest answer is, both.

123
00:04:52,574 --> 00:04:55,229
And the reason why is the weirdest part

124
00:04:55,229 --> 00:04:56,590
of this whole story.

125
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:59,951
Your brain seems to be catnapping in pieces.

126
00:04:59,951 --> 00:05:02,763
Some parts asleep, other parts awake, taking

127
00:05:02,763 --> 00:05:05,446
turns. One of your friends later says your

128
00:05:05,446 --> 00:05:08,130
brain had been catnapping the entire time.

129
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:10,234
It's not a superpower.

130
00:05:10,234 --> 00:05:13,342
It's damage control. You're not really awake

131
00:05:13,342 --> 00:05:16,380
anymore. You're just not completely asleep.

132
00:05:16,930 --> 00:05:19,869
Day six. Your immune system is running on

133
00:05:19,869 --> 00:05:22,736
fumes too. Sleep is when a lot of immune

134
00:05:22,736 --> 00:05:24,170
maintenance happens.

135
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:26,972
In studies, people who skipped sleep the

136
00:05:26,972 --> 00:05:29,281
night after a vaccine often made a weaker

137
00:05:29,281 --> 00:05:30,519
immune response to it.

138
00:05:30,519 --> 00:05:32,715
Your body is too busy surviving tonight

139
00:05:32,715 --> 00:05:34,010
to plan for next month.

140
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:36,798
And your memory? You're forgetting what

141
00:05:36,798 --> 00:05:38,061
you said a minute ago.

142
00:05:38,061 --> 00:05:40,356
You're losing the thread halfway through

143
00:05:40,356 --> 00:05:40,930
sentences.

144
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:44,255
Meanwhile, outside, you've become famous.

145
00:05:44,255 --> 00:05:46,963
For a short time, your experiment is the

146
00:05:46,963 --> 00:05:50,077
third most written about story in the American

147
00:05:50,077 --> 00:05:52,717
press, behind the Kennedy assassination

148
00:05:52,717 --> 00:05:53,800
and the Beatles.

149
00:05:54,350 --> 00:05:57,123
So, day eight. Can this actually kill you?

150
00:05:57,123 --> 00:05:58,840
Here's where it gets dark.

151
00:05:59,390 --> 00:06:01,989
In the nineteen eighties, researchers kept

152
00:06:01,989 --> 00:06:04,526
rats awake on a turning disc above water.

153
00:06:04,526 --> 00:06:07,372
Every single rat died, or was put down because

154
00:06:07,372 --> 00:06:10,281
death looked close, within eleven to thirty-two

155
00:06:10,281 --> 00:06:10,590
days.

156
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:13,531
They ate more food than ever and still lost

157
00:06:13,531 --> 00:06:15,755
weight. Their bodies were burning energy

158
00:06:15,755 --> 00:06:17,702
at more than twice the normal rate.

159
00:06:17,702 --> 00:06:19,926
And the scientists couldn't find a clear

160
00:06:19,926 --> 00:06:20,760
cause of death.

161
00:06:21,310 --> 00:06:24,002
It took until twenty twenty to find a big

162
00:06:24,002 --> 00:06:26,695
clue. In fruit flies and mice, sleep loss

163
00:06:26,695 --> 00:06:29,650
caused a build up of harmful molecules called

164
00:06:29,650 --> 00:06:32,408
reactive oxygen species. Not in the brain.

165
00:06:32,408 --> 00:06:33,130
In the gut.

166
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:36,103
And when the scientists neutralized those

167
00:06:36,103 --> 00:06:38,703
molecules with antioxidants, the flies lived

168
00:06:38,703 --> 00:06:41,185
a normal lifespan with little to no sleep.

169
00:06:41,185 --> 00:06:43,430
Please do not try this with smoothies.

170
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:46,712
In humans, there's a rare genetic disease

171
00:06:46,712 --> 00:06:49,444
called fatal familial insomnia, where the

172
00:06:49,444 --> 00:06:52,310
brain gradually loses the ability to sleep.

173
00:06:52,310 --> 00:06:55,442
People with it die, on average, around eighteen

174
00:06:55,442 --> 00:06:57,307
months after symptoms start.

175
00:06:57,307 --> 00:06:58,840
There is no known cure.

176
00:06:59,390 --> 00:07:02,054
Which is why, after people kept trying to

177
00:07:02,054 --> 00:07:04,652
beat your record, Guinness World Records

178
00:07:04,652 --> 00:07:07,640
stopped certifying sleep deprivation attempts.

179
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:08,550
Too dangerous.

180
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:11,824
Day eleven. Your attention keeps slipping.

181
00:07:11,824 --> 00:07:14,614
One scientist later describes you as awake,

182
00:07:14,614 --> 00:07:16,300
but basically not working.

183
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:18,450
And then, it's done.

184
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:21,330
Eleven days and twenty-five minutes.

185
00:07:21,330 --> 00:07:23,970
Two hundred and sixty-four hours.

186
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:26,050
The DJ's record is broken.

187
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:29,042
There's a press conference outside your

188
00:07:29,042 --> 00:07:31,546
parents' house. Someone asks how you did

189
00:07:31,546 --> 00:07:34,050
it. You say, it's just mind over matter.

190
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,054
Then they take you to a naval hospital,

191
00:07:37,054 --> 00:07:39,382
wire up your head, and let you sleep.

192
00:07:39,382 --> 00:07:42,151
You sleep for about fourteen hours and forty

193
00:07:42,151 --> 00:07:44,793
minutes. And then you wake up, because you

194
00:07:44,793 --> 00:07:45,800
need the toilet.

195
00:07:46,350 --> 00:07:48,998
On that first night, the share of dream

196
00:07:48,998 --> 00:07:50,492
sleep, REM, shoots up.

197
00:07:50,492 --> 00:07:53,208
Your brain is paying back its debt, with

198
00:07:53,208 --> 00:07:55,992
interest. The next nights it settles back

199
00:07:55,992 --> 00:07:57,010
down to normal.

200
00:07:57,560 --> 00:08:00,524
And then, unbelievably, you get up and go

201
00:08:00,524 --> 00:08:01,970
back to high school.

202
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:05,028
You seem fine. But decades later, Randy

203
00:08:05,028 --> 00:08:06,700
says the price came later.

204
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:08,243
Years of awful insomnia.

205
00:08:08,243 --> 00:08:10,815
The boy who stayed awake for eleven days

206
00:08:10,815 --> 00:08:13,130
ended up struggling to sleep at all.

207
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:15,140
Now look at your own week.

208
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:17,387
Six hours a night feels normal for a lot

209
00:08:17,387 --> 00:08:19,578
of people. But in lab studies, sleeping

210
00:08:19,578 --> 00:08:21,824
six hours a night for two weeks built up

211
00:08:21,824 --> 00:08:24,071
deficits in attention and memory similar

212
00:08:24,071 --> 00:08:26,430
to one to three nights of no sleep at all.

213
00:08:26,980 --> 00:08:28,296
And the worst part?

214
00:08:28,296 --> 00:08:31,066
People on six hours stopped feeling much

215
00:08:31,066 --> 00:08:33,421
sleepier after the first few days.

216
00:08:33,421 --> 00:08:35,430
The tests kept getting worse.

217
00:08:35,430 --> 00:08:37,300
They just stopped noticing.

218
00:08:37,850 --> 00:08:40,702
So here's your prescription from this channel.

219
00:08:40,702 --> 00:08:43,182
Sleep tonight. It's the cheapest upgrade

220
00:08:43,182 --> 00:08:44,670
your body will ever get.

221
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:47,258
And if you're watching this at three in

222
00:08:47,258 --> 00:08:49,295
the morning, I mean, I can't judge you.

223
00:08:49,295 --> 00:08:51,385
You're the one who clicked a sleep video

224
00:08:51,385 --> 00:08:52,430
instead of sleeping.

225
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:54,824
Next week, you fall into the deepest place

226
00:08:54,824 --> 00:08:56,623
on Earth, and we follow your body all the

227
00:08:56,623 --> 00:08:58,423
way down. Eleven kilometres of water, and

228
00:08:58,423 --> 00:08:59,564
one very bad swimming day.

229
00:08:59,564 --> 00:09:00,530
See you at the bottom.
